      <item>
         <title><![CDATA[Ottawa companies look to break Starlink's grip on Arctic military communications]]></title>
         <link>https://www.cbc.ca/news/politics/canada-arctic-starlink-musk-satellites-9.7428908?cmp=rss</link>
         <description><![CDATA[<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/ebf12314-f8bc-4d0b-90aa-b812d63cfbc5,1795129526649/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C169%2C5000%2C2812%29%3B' alt='A man gestures while wearing a headset.' width='620' height='349' title='Dominion Dynamics founder Eliot Pence speaks at the 2026 Toronto Tech Week Homecoming conference in Toronto, on Wednesday, May 27, 2026. THE CANADIAN PRESS/Sammy Kogan'/><p>Two Canadian high-tech firms have taken a step toward challenging Elon Musk's Starlink with plans for a homegrown military satellite communications system. Dominion Dynamics has signed a $2.2-million deal with Telesat to develop Arctic-ready technology, part of a broader effort to strengthen Canadian sovereignty and develop an uncrewed combat aircraft.</p>]]></description>
         <pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 04:00:00 EST</pubDate>
